              "inline_data": null,
              "text": "That whitish film is a very common occurrence, and it is almost certainly caused by one of two things (or a combination of both): **mineral deposits** and **biofilm**. \n\nHere is a breakdown of what is happening to your glass, why your fridge filter isn't stopping it, and how to easily prevent and clean it.\n\n---\n\n### 1. Hard Water Mineral Deposits (Limescale)\nEven though you are using filtered water from your fridge, standard refrigerator water filters (which typically use activated carbon) are designed to remove bad tastes, odors, chlorine, and certain chemical contaminants. They **do not** remove dissolved minerals like calcium and magnesium. \n\nOnly specialized systems, like reverse osmosis (RO) filters or dedicated water softeners, actually strip these minerals out of the water.\n\n* **How it happens:** Every time you take a sip or move the glass, water coats the inside walls. As the water evaporates from the glass's surface, it leaves behind the dissolved calcium and magnesium. Over the course of a week, these microscopic layers build up on top of one another, forming a cloudy, chalky white film.\n\n### 2. Biofilm and Saliva Residue\nBecause you are using the same glass for \"a week or so\" without washing it, organic buildup is also a major culprit. \n\n* **How it happens:** Every time your lips touch the glass, you transfer saliva, skin cells, and natural mouth bacteria into the water and onto the rim. Additionally, dust and airborne yeast or bacteria land in the water. \n* Over a few days, these microorganisms feed on those trace organic materials and build a microscopic colony called a **biofilm**. Biofilms often start as a clear, slimy layer but can dry into a cloudy, hazy, or whitish film on the glass.\n\n---\n\n### How to Tell the Difference (The Vinegar Test)\nTo find out exactly what you are dealing with, try this quick test:\n* Put a splash of **white vinegar** on a paper towel and wipe the dry glass. \n* **If the film dissolves immediately:** It is mineral buildup. The acid in the vinegar neutralizes and dissolves the alkaline calcium carbonate.\n* **If it resists the vinegar but scrubs off easily with regular dish soap:** It is likely organic biofilm and saliva residue.\n\n### How to Clean and Prevent It\n1. **To clean it:** If regular washing doesn't get the glass perfectly clear, soak it in a mixture of 50/50 warm water and white vinegar for 15 minutes, then wipe it clean with a soft sponge.\n2. **Wash the glass more frequently:** Even though it \"only has water in it,\" bacteria from your mouth and the air thrive in stagnant moisture. It is highly recommended to wash your drinking glasses with soap and warm water every 1 to 2 days to keep bacteria and biofilm from developing.",
